<Project>
    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <!-- ⛔ THE SINGULAR FORM IS LOAD-BEARING, and two projects once believed otherwise.
             src/ClaudeForge and LayeredEditors.Avalonia.Services (since plans/00005 the AppServices
             packages) each declared a plural
             <TargetFrameworks> adding net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0, with comments saying it took
             precedence over this property. It does not: MSBuild cross-targets only when
             TargetFramework is EMPTY, so both projects built net10.0 alone for the whole life of
             those declarations, and the MAUI Essentials share path they existed for never
             compiled. Both declarations are now gone.

             Adding a platform TFM to any project therefore means CLEARING this property for it
             first — `<TargetFramework></TargetFramework>` in that csproj, before the plural form
             — and re-adding EnableWindowsTargeting, which used to sit here so a Windows TFM
             would restore on macOS / Linux CI hosts without NETSDK1100. Neither is needed while
             every project is single-TFM, and leaving them in place documents a capability the
             build does not have. -->
    </PropertyGroup>
    
    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- Turn the generator ON for the consuming projects -->
        <GenerateAutoVersionedAssemblyInfo>true</GenerateAutoVersionedAssemblyInfo>

        <!-- Company and product names; consumed by the source generator at build time -->
        <AssemblyCompany>Bennewitz.Ninja</AssemblyCompany>
        <!--
          The DEFAULT product, which is now the shared library family rather than either app.

          ⚠ This used to be `ClaudeForge`, which stamped that name into every assembly in the
          repository — including AgentForge.* and JsonC, which are product-neutral by
          design and are consumed by both apps. A shared library reporting itself as one of its
          two consumers is wrong in a file properties dialog, in a crash dump, and in any
          support bundle that lists loaded modules.

          Each app OVERRIDES this in its own csproj with its own name. Adding a third app means
          adding the same one-line override there, not editing this.
        -->
        <AssemblyProduct>AgentForge</AssemblyProduct>
        <CopyrightHolder>Brian Bennewitz</CopyrightHolder>

        <!-- Map CI environment variables to the MSBuild properties the generator reads.
             Replace $(GITHUB_SHA) / $(GITHUB_ACTIONS) with your CI provider's equivalents,
             e.g. GitLab: $(CI_COMMIT_SHA) / $(GITLAB_CI), Bitbucket: $(BITBUCKET_COMMIT) / $(CI). -->
        <CommitSha Condition="'$(CommitSha)' == ''">$(GITHUB_SHA)</CommitSha>
        <IsContinuousIntegration>$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)</IsContinuousIntegration>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      ══ THE VERSION OF THE SIX SHARED PACKAGES THE RELEASE CONSUMES ═════════════════════════

      plans/00003, Phase D. This is a COMMITTED PIN, deliberately, and it belongs in the ROOT
      props rather than src/'s: the test projects consume the packages too, and only the root
      file reaches them.

      ⭐ WHY A PIN AND NOT A DERIVED VALUE. The app release tag and the package tag are
      independent — 'v2026.3.918' and 'packages-v2026.3.918' are separate releases on separate
      schedules — so nothing about an app tag says which package version it should consume. An
      explicit value also satisfies Directory.Build.targets' refusal to invent a default, makes
      the release reproducible from its tag with no workflow inputs, and turns every version
      move into a diff a human approves.

      ⚠ THIS IS INERT UNTIL UseSharedPackages=true. In the default development mode nothing
      reads it, the feed is never contacted, and a clone with no credentials restores normally.
      The canary passes its own throwaway version on the command line, and a global property
      still outranks this one.

      ⛔ BUMPING THIS IS A RELEASE ACTION. It must name a version that actually exists on the
      feed: a value pointing at an unpublished version fails the restore rather than falling
      back, which is the intended behaviour.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <SharedPackageVersion Condition="'$(SharedPackageVersion)' == ''">2026.3.925</SharedPackageVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      ══ THE SEVEN nuget.org PACKAGES THAT REPLACE LayeredEditors ════════════════════════════

      plans/00005. TWO properties because the seven ids come from two repositories with
      independent release cadences: Bennewitz.Ninja.AppServices (four ids) and
      Bennewitz.Ninja.ScopedEditors (three). One version per repository, from one source, so
      moving a pin is a one-line diff a human approves.

      ⛔ UNLIKE SharedPackageVersion ABOVE, THESE ARE NEVER INERT. The seven are PackageReferences
      in every mode; there is no project to fall back to, so every restore reads them.

      ⛔ THE PIN MUST CARRY THE IsTrimmable MARK. ClaudeForge publishes with TrimMode=partial,
      which trims and analyses only marked assemblies. 2026.3.923 shipped without it, so there
      the seven were kept whole and a clean trim result said nothing about them; 2026.3.924
      carries it on all seven (read from the DLLs). Check before moving the pin. See plans/00005.

      No Condition, deliberately: the value in this file must beat a same-named environment
      variable, and a global property on the command line still outranks it.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <AppServicesVersion>2026.3.924</AppServicesVersion>
        <ScopedEditorsVersion>2026.3.924</ScopedEditorsVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      PDB policy — applies to all projects in the solution unless a project overrides.
  
      Debug:   portable PDB (separate .pdb file alongside the assembly).
               Portable format is cross-platform and required for local source-stepping;
               the separate file is expected and convenient in the debug output directory.
  
      Release: embedded PDB (debug info baked directly into the managed assembly).
               The embedded format is the portable format inlined as a PE section;
               no separate .pdb file is produced, so there are no loose symbol files
               to exclude from zips or installers.  When PublishSingleFile bundles
               an assembly into the single-file executable the embedded PDB travels
               with it automatically — giving full symbols inside the shipped binary
               with zero extra files.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">
        <DebugType>portable</DebugType>
        <DebugSymbols>true</DebugSymbols>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
        <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
        <DebugSymbols>true</DebugSymbols>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- AutoVersioning source generator — applies to every project in the solution.
         Hoisted here because the previous per-csproj pattern duplicated the
         identical PackageReference block across 13 projects (52 lines of noise).
         The generator is gated by GenerateAutoVersionedAssemblyInfo=true above,
         so it actually runs on every project; this single declaration is all
         that's needed.

         PrivateAssets="all" keeps the generator from flowing transitively to
         consumers of any library this repo ships (it's a build-time tool, not
         a runtime dependency).  IncludeAssets enumerates the asset types the
         generator legitimately needs at build time. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Bennewitz.Ninja.AutoVersioning" Version="2026.3.916">
            <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
            <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
        </PackageReference>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
